.Dd November 8, 2026
.Dt NEXTBOOT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nextboot
.Nd "specify an alternate kernel and boot flags for the next reboot"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl af
.Op Fl e Ar variable=value
.Op Fl k Ar kernel
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Nm
.Fl D
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility allows specifying some combination of an alternate kernel, boot
flags, and kernel environment for the next time the machine is booted.
The
.Xr loader 8
reads the settings from
.Pa /boot/nextboot.conf
and marks the file as used before the kernel is loaded, so if the new
kernel hangs the machine, the next boot reverts to the previous
configuration.
.Pp
A manual selection from the loader menu wins over
.Nm :
the entry is still marked as used, but it is not applied.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width ".Fl o Ar options"
.It Fl a
Append to an existing configuration in
.Pa /boot/nextboot.conf
instead of replacing it.
The enabling line is written first, so a file that an earlier boot has
consumed is applied as well.
.It Fl D
Remove an existing
.Nm
configuration.
It cannot be combined with any other option.
.It Fl e Ar variable=value
Set
.Va variable
to
.Va value
for the next boot.
The assignment is applied like a
.Pa loader.conf
line: tunables (names containing a dot) and variables that the loader
exports are placed in the kernel environment, other names stay loader
variables.
The value is quoted when it is written to the
.Nm
configuration.
.It Fl f
Disable the sanity checks.
Without this flag, a kernel directory that does not exist and a
.Pa /boot
that is not a UFS file system are rejected.
With this flag those checks and write failures only produce warnings
and
.Nm
exits 0.
.It Fl k Ar kernel
Load the kernel and modules from the directory
.Ar kernel
relative to
.Pa /boot
on the next boot.
.It Fl o Ar options
Pass
.Ar options
to the kernel as
.Va kernel_options
on the next boot.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /boot/nextboot.conf" -compact
.It Pa /boot/nextboot.conf
The configuration file that the
.Nm
configuration is written into.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
To boot the
.Pa kernel.test
kernel with the
.Nm
command:
.Pp
.Dl "nextboot -k kernel.test"
.Pp
To boot the normal kernel into single user mode:
.Pp
.Dl "nextboot -o ""-s"""
.Pp
To remove an existing nextboot configuration:
.Pp
.Dl "nextboot -D"
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr boot 8 ,
.Xr loader 8 ,
.Xr reboot 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Fx 2.2
with a different interface.
The current interface appeared in
.Fx 5.0 .
This integration of
.Nm
into
.Xr reboot 8
follows the current
.Fx
design.
.Sh AUTHORS
This manual page was written by
.An Gordon Tetlow Aq Mt gordon@FreeBSD.org .
.Sh BUGS
The
.Nm
code is implemented in the
.Xr loader 8 ,
which can only mark the entry as used on a UFS
.Pa /boot
and only on media the firmware can write.
A
.Pa /boot
on another file system is refused by this tool; on media whose block
size the firmware reports as something other than 512 bytes (some EFI
disks), the
.Xr loader 8
cannot mark the entry as used and ignores the file entirely, so the
boot proceeds normally.
.Pp
Only
.Va variable Ns = Ns Va value
lines are read from the file; the
.Va loader_conf_files
variable is not supported.
A configuration larger than 1024 bytes is rejected, because the loader
applies only that much.
